INSERT INTO `exercises` VALUES (5,'{\"text\": \"We\'ve all felt it: that uncomfortable feeling when you scroll through your social media feed and see photos of friends having a better time than you, or that sensation when you read about a friend\'s amazing job that you chose the wrong life path. This feeling is called FOMO, or fear of missing out. The term was first coined in 1996 by Marketing Strategist Dr. Dan Herman. While listening to consumers in focus groups and interviews, he observed that many consumers mentioned a fear of missing out on opportunities which could bring them pleasure. Later, in 2004, Patrick McGinnis, a student at Harvard Business School, co-edited an article about the growing trend among his peers of being unable to commit to anything, even something as simple as booking a restaurant, for fear that they would miss out on something more exciting happening elsewhere.\\n\\nAlthough people have felt FOMO for time immemorial, the growth of social media seems to have exacerbated the phenomenon. For many, it has now become habit to compare your life with others\' lives - or rather the highlights of their lives; something that previous generations could not do so readily. This skews your sense of normal and brings about feelings like resentment, envy and dissatisfaction. What\'s more, marketers have seized on FOMO psychology as a means to drive sales. Sales that last a limited time, low stock availability notifications and pop-ups that show other people buying all tap into our FOMO.\\n\\nSince the FOMO phenomenon was recognised, it has been increasingly studied by scientists eager to discover its trends and impacts. Scientists at Carleton and McGill University, for example, found that, surprisingly, traits like neuroticism and extroversion did not lead to a greater incidence of FOMO. They did, however, discover that negative FOMO feelings were experienced more often towards the end of the day and at the end of the week and that FOMO was experienced more by people carrying out obligatory work, like jobs and study.\\n\\nThis is not to say that people only experience FOMO when engaged in a mundane activity. In a follow-up study, researchers found that participants who specifically selected one activity over another experienced FOMO when reminded about the alternative activity, even if their chosen activity was sociable and enjoyable, and if the alternative was unsociable. Moreover, they experienced FOMO whether they were reminded about the alternative through social media or in conversation.\\n\\nInterestingly, although FOMO is widely associated with teenagers and young adults and those who use social media, research has found that people of all ages experience it, irrespective of their social media use. Researchers at Washington State University, found that it is more closely linked to factors like loneliness and low self-esteem. However, for those people, social media can exacerbate the problem.\\n\\nSome psychologists recognise an upside to FOMO, saying that it can motivate you to take action, connect with others and get out of your comfort zone. More often than not, though, FOMO leads to increasing isolation and even FOJI, fear of joining in, in the belief that your own insights or contributions will not be valued.\\n\\nA rising counter-culture to FOMO, though, is JOMO - the joy of missing out. This includes the pleasure and satisfaction of a night in, doing what you enjoy best, turning off your phone notifications and living in the moment, focusing not on what you lack but on what you have.\", \"title\": \"Fear Of Missing Out (FOMO)\", \"instruction\": \"Read the text carefully before answering the questions.\"}',1,'Text',1),(6,'{\"answer\": \"C\", \"options\": [\"A: in a paper published by a Harvard student\", \"B: on social media\", \"C: by someone doing market research\"], \"question\": \"The term FOMO was first used...\", \"explanation\": \"The text states the term was coined in 1996 by Marketing Strategist Dr. Dan Herman while listening to consumers in focus groups.\"}',2,'Option',1),(7,'{\"answer\": \"B\", \"options\": [\"A: is the same as in 2004.\", \"B: has changed since 2004.\", \"C: changed between 1996 and 2004.\"], \"question\": \"It can be inferred that the meaning of FOMO now...\", \"explanation\": \"In 2004, the term referred specifically to an inability to commit to plans; the text suggests it has since evolved to include social media comparison and marketing exploitation.\"}',3,'Option',1),(8,'{\"answer\": \"C\", \"options\": [\"A: It can alter your perception of what normal is.\", \"B: It can be exploited to make people spend money.\", \"C: It can make people fearful of normal social interactions.\"], \"question\": \"Which of the following impacts of FOMO is NOT mentioned in the text?\", \"explanation\": \"While the text mentions isolation and FOJI (fear of joining in), it specifies that FOJI is the belief that one\'s contributions won\'t be valued, rather than a general fear of social interactions.\"}',4,'Option',1),(9,'{\"answer\": \"B\", \"options\": [\"A: marketing campaigns were less targeted towards them.\", \"B: they were less aware of what others were doing.\", \"C: their lives were more similar to those of their peers.\"], \"question\": \"According to the text, people in previous generations experienced FOMO less than nowadays because...\", \"explanation\": \"The text notes that previous generations could not readily compare their lives with the highlights of others\' lives as social media users do today.\"}',5,'Option',1),(10,'{\"answer\": \"A\", \"options\": [\"A: an introvert doing school work on a Friday night.\", \"B: a neurotic person relaxing on a Sunday afternoon.\", \"C: an extrovert working on a Tuesday morning.\"], \"question\": \"According to the research, which of these people is MOST likely to experience FOMO?\", \"explanation\": \"Research found FOMO is highest at the end of the week (Friday) and while performing obligatory work (school work), regardless of personality traits like extroversion or neuroticism.\"}',6,'Option',1),(12,'{\"text\": \"Right now, somewhere in the world, Tom Turcich is walking. He started his hike in April 2015 and is still going. His intention is to to walk across every continent in the world.\\n\\nTom made the decision to travel the world after one of his close friends died at the age of 17. The tragedy encouraged Tom to make the most of his own life. He spent the next few years completing college and working to save money, and in 2015, he finally quit his job, packed a few belongings and set off on foot from his home in New Jersey, USA.\\n\\nHe took a route south through the USA and Central America and down the west coast of South America. After that, he travelled to Antarctica and Europe aboard a ship, and he is currently walking across North Africa. From there, he\'ll hike east through Asia and Australia. Then he plans to fly to the west coast of the USA and cross that on foot, back towards his final destination, his home in New Jersey.\\n\\nTom isn\'t walking alone. He picked up a dog called Savannah from an animal shelter in Texas. At first, he thought it would be useful to have a dog protecting him, but the two soon became perfect travel companions. He thought he would have trouble crossing borders with the dog, but he acquired a certificate proving that it was free of disease and since then the process has been surprisingly simple.\\n\\nTurcich usually walks about 38 kilometres a day, burning 5000 calories daily. He has encountered a number of difficulties on the way, such as walking across the mountains and volcanoes of Guatemala. They were so steep that he could only manage about 10 miles a day. He carries just a tent, a sleeping bag and a few basic supplies, all of which he pushes a baby stroller, as he found that it requires less effort than carrying a backpack.\\n\\nWhen he first started out, his legs ached at the end of the day. But he is now used to the exercise, although he makes sure to spend time at the end of the day stretching his legs. He had worn out several pairs of hiking boots already, each pair lasting about 800 kilometres. Tom has an arrangement with a friend in the States who mails him new pairs of sneakers whenever he needs them.\\n\\nHe has had some great experiences along the way and is always surprised by the generosity and hospitality of strangers, who frequently welcome him into their homes. However, few of them believe him when he tells them that he has walked to South America on foot, assuming that he has made a mistake speaking Spanish. But eventually, they accept his story and are extremely impressed.\\n\\nTom keeps a blog where he documents his travels and posts wonderful photographs that he has taken along the way. He had never been interested in photography before he started his trek but now loves the art form. He hopes that after returning to the USA, he will be able to work as a travel writer or photographer.\", \"title\": \"World walker\", \"instruction\": \"Read the text carefully before answering the questions.\"}',1,'Text',5),(14,'{\"answer\": \"False\", \"options\": [\"True\", \"False\"], \"question\": \"Tom set off on his journey right after his friend died.\", \"explanation\": \"He spent several years completing college and saving money before starting.\"}',2,'True_False',5),(15,'{\"answer\": \"True\", \"options\": [\"True\", \"False\"], \"question\": \"Tom has already walked across South America and Europe.\"}',3,'True_False',5),(16,'{\"answer\": \"False\", \"options\": [\"True\", \"False\"], \"question\": \"He took with him a dog that he\'d had for years.\"}',4,'True_False',5),(17,'{\"answer\": \"True\", \"options\": [\"True\", \"False\"], \"question\": \"In Guatemala, he had to slow down his pace.\"}',5,'True_False',5),(18,'{\"answer\": \"True\", \"options\": [\"True\", \"False\"], \"question\": \"He thinks a baby stroller is more convenient than a backpack.\"}',6,'True_False',5),(19,'{\"text\": \"Binge-watching has become a popular way of consuming television and movie content, with streaming services making it easier than ever to marathon entire seasons or movie franchises in a single sitting. With the growing number of original series and the convenience of on-demand viewing, binge-watching has transformed how we watch television and has become a ubiquitous part of popular culture.\\n\\nThis week, we wanted to provide a unique look into the different experiences and perspectives on binge-watching within families. So, we talked to various members of the Evans family to discuss their habits and attitudes towards this trend.\\n\\n13-year-old Amelia admits to being a huge binge-watcher. She says, \'For me, it\'s an important part of fitting in. My friend Denise isn\'t allowed to watch loads of television at home, and that makes joining in conversations really hard\'. So now, Denise comes to the Evans house to binge-watch. Amelia admits that she doesn\'t do much besides watching films and series. Once keen on ballet, she gave that up years ago and hasn\'t picked up anything else since. However, Amelia knows that her grades at school are crucial in determining her ability to continue her TV-viewing habits.\\n\\nHer mother, Karen, realises that Amelia is getting into a bad habit, but hasn\'t done anything about it. \'If I stopped her, then I would have to cut down on the amount that I watch to set a good example, and to be honest, I don\'t want to,\' she says. \'After a hard day doing work and chores, who doesn\'t love chilling out for a few hours with some episodes of a good drama? Besides, it\'s not like I could do anything else instead, like join an evening class. I rarely get a chance to sit down before eight o\'clock\'.\\n\\nKyle, aged 17, usually has a video playing in his room. \'I wouldn\'t call myself a binge-watcher, though,\' he says. \'I\'m not one of those people who watch all the latest series as soon as they are released. For me, it\'s just background noise while I\'m getting on with my hobbies. I just let it play whatever comes up next. I\'ve seen all sorts of interesting stuff that I\'d never choose for myself. I once saw a guy teaching how to cut up different fish. That was really cool.\'\\n\\nFather, Rob, sees it in financial terms. \'The monthly fee for streaming services is quite expensive, but in terms of the amount of entertainment we get, it\'s good value. And the more you watch, the cheaper it gets. It\'s much less than going out to the cinema, and everyone can watch whatever they like. Plus, even though movies or series are often bad, you can just switch to something else. Some of my friends have stopped paying for streaming services recently, saying they\'re too expensive. That\'s their loss\'.\\n\\nRob\'s mother, Gill, is less enthusiastic, despite once enjoying watching back-to-back episodes of her favourite classic drama while bedridden due to illness. \'I remember having to wait a whole week for the next episode of that series to come out,\' she says. \'And months for the next series. You got the excitement of wondering what was going to happen next. Nowadays, you get everything as soon as you want it, not like old-fashioned TV, and I think you lose something as a result\'.\\n\\nMeanwhile, Karen\'s brother Kevin is interested in the intelligent design of streaming platforms. \'They make recommendations based on what you\'ve enjoyed, and often the recommendations are right up your street, so I guess that encourages binge-watching. Some people say that this limits what you watch. I\'m not sure I agree, though. As a sci-fi fan, live-streaming services have introduced me to some great foreign and low-budget movies and series that I wouldn\'t have known about. I\'m sure that up-and-coming filmmakers worldwide are benefiting from that promotion.\'\", \"title\": \"Binge-watching\", \"instruction\": \"Read the text carefully before answering the questions.\"}',1,'Text',9),(20,'{\"answer\": \"B\", \"options\": [\"A: Because she prefers the series Amelia chooses.\", \"B: Because she is not permitted to watch much television at her own home.\", \"C: Because she and Amelia are studying for school together.\"], \"question\": \"Why does Denise watch television at Amelia\'s house?\", \"explanation\": \"The text states Denise isn\'t allowed to watch loads of television at home, which makes joining in conversations hard, so she goes to Amelia\'s.\"}',2,'Option',9),(21,'{\"answer\": \"A\", \"options\": [\"A: Because she doesn\'t want to have to limit her own TV time as an example.\", \"B: Because she thinks Amelia needs it to relax after ballet.\", \"C: Because she wants Amelia to fit in with friends like Denise.\"], \"question\": \"Why has Karen decided not to stop Amelia\'s binge-watching habit?\", \"explanation\": \"Karen admits that if she stopped Amelia, she would have to cut down on the amount she watches to set a good example, and she doesn\'t want to do that.\"}',3,'Option',9),(22,'{\"answer\": \"C\", \"options\": [\"A: He only watches videos about his specific hobbies.\", \"B: He watches every new series the moment it is released.\", \"C: He treats the videos as background noise while doing other things.\"], \"question\": \"What is unique about Kyle\'s way of watching videos?\", \"explanation\": \"Kyle says it is just background noise while he is getting on with his hobbies and he lets it play whatever comes up next.\"}',4,'Option',9),(23,'{\"answer\": \"B\", \"options\": [\"A: He thinks they are becoming too expensive for most families.\", \"B: He believes they offer better value for money than going to the cinema.\", \"C: He feels the quality of the movies is too low for the monthly fee.\"], \"question\": \"What is Rob\'s opinion on the cost of streaming services?\", \"explanation\": \"Rob mentions it is much less than going out to the cinema and that for the amount of entertainment they get, it is good value.\"}',5,'Option',9),(24,'{\"answer\": \"C\", \"options\": [\"A: The quality of the acting in classic dramas.\", \"B: The ability to watch television when you are feeling unwell.\", \"C: The excitement of waiting for a new episode to be released.\"], \"question\": \"What does Gill think is lost with modern streaming services?\", \"explanation\": \"Gill says that in the past, waiting a week for the next episode provided the excitement of wondering what was going to happen next.\"}',6,'Option',9),(25,'{\"title\": \"Culture shock: Living abroad\", \"videoUrl\": \"https://www.youtube.com/embed/WH66_Fjp7Dw\", \"instruction\": \"Watch the video and answer the question below:\"}',1,'Video',2),(31,'{\"answer\": \"False\", \"options\": [\"True\", \"False\"], \"question\": \"Andrew\'s landlord sent him an apology letter.\"}',2,'True_False',2),(32,'{\"answer\": \"True\", \"options\": [\"True\", \"False\"], \"question\": \"Andrew isn\'t good at wrapping gifts.\"}',3,'True_False',2),(33,'{\"answer\": \"False\", \"options\": [\"True\", \"False\"], \"question\": \"Erica wasn\'t the typical Brit when she first arrived in Brazil.\"}',4,'True_False',2),(34,'{\"answer\": \"True\", \"options\": [\"True\", \"False\"], \"question\": \"Now, Erica has to make an effort when she\'s in England.\"}',5,'True_False',2),(35,'{\"answer\": \"True\", \"options\": [\"True\", \"False\"], \"question\": \"April went to Vietnam because she wanted to change her life.\"}',6,'True_False',2),(39,'{\"text\": \"Hey! That\'s great! I (1) ____ prefer Monday afternoon because it isn\'t (2) ____ for me, as I have tennis classes after school. On Friday I have (3) ____ to do, so that day is perfect! My favorite (4) ____ of films are comedy and romantic movies. I think (5) ____ a very good idea to provide snacks. I can (6) ____ some popcorn and a bottle of water. See you soon, Clara!\", \"title\": \"Clara\'s Email Reply\", \"answer_key\": {\"1\": \"don\'t\", \"2\": \"possible\", \"3\": \"nothing\", \"4\": \"types\", \"5\": \"it\'s\", \"6\": \"bring\"}, \"instruction\": \"Read Clara\'s reply answering a film club invitation and choose the correct word for each gap.\", \"requirements\": {\"1\": [\"don\'t\", \"doesn\'t\", \"am not\", \"not\"], \"2\": [\"possible\", \"positive\", \"ready\", \"open\"], \"3\": [\"nothing\", \"anything\", \"something\", \"everything\"], \"4\": [\"types\", \"kinds\", \"sets\", \"parts\"], \"5\": [\"it\'s\", \"its\", \"is\", \"there\"], \"6\": [\"bring\", \"buy\", \"take\", \"give\"]}}',1,'FillGaps',3),(43,'{\"title\": \"Speaking test: sample\", \"videoUrl\": \"https://www.youtube.com/embed/xF_Q2anYOfc\", \"instruction\": \"Watch this sample speaking test carefully. Pay close attention to the candidate\'s fluency, their use of varied vocabulary, and how they structure their responses to the examiner\'s prompts. Observe the body language and the interaction between both parties to better understand the pacing and formal expectations of the assessment.\"}',1,'Video',4),(45,'{\"answer\": \"True\", \"options\": [\"True\", \"False\"], \"question\": \"Did you watch the video?\"}',2,'True_False',4),(46,'{\"title\": \"Air travel experiences\", \"videoUrl\": \"https://www.youtube.com/embed/c6KXFAXULgs\", \"instruction\": \"Watch the video and answer the questions below:\"}',1,'Video',10),(47,'{\"title\": \"Surviving in the jungle\", \"videoUrl\": \"https://www.youtube.com/embed/lpnB6dW_sso\", \"instruction\": \"Watch the video and answer the questions below:\"}',1,'Video',6),(48,'{\"answer\": \"A\", \"options\": [\"A: because you need to protect yourself from insects.\", \"B: so that you have time to find food.\", \"C: because you need to prepare since the floor is too wet to sleep on.\"], \"question\": \"In the jungle you need to find a place to camp early ...\"}',2,'Option',6),(49,'{\"answer\": \"A\", \"options\": [\"A: there’s a risk that rain sweeps him into the river.\", \"B: rivers are infested with mosquitoes.\", \"C: he could be dragged by one of the river creatures.\"], \"question\": \"Mike wants to camp close to the river but not too close because...\"}',3,'Option',6),(50,'{\"answer\": \"B\", \"options\": [\"A: magnifying lenses are totally useless in the jungle.\", \"B: it\'s the only way to light a fire without difficulty in the jungle.\", \"C: only survival experts can easily light a fire in the jungle without one.\"], \"question\": \"It’s essential to carry a lighter because ...\"}',4,'Option',6),(51,'{\"answer\": \"C\", \"options\": [\"A: its flavour is better\", \"B: it’s safer to eat cooked food\", \"C: for both reasons\"], \"question\": \"Mike cooks food in the fire because ...\"}',5,'Option',6),(52,'{\"answer\": \"C\", \"options\": [\"A: cooks a lot of food and eats everything.\", \"B: eats a little and saves a lot of food for the following days.\", \"C: eats as much as he can but also saves some food.\"], \"question\": \"Mike ....\"}',6,'Option',6),(54,'{\"answer\": \"C\", \"options\": [\"A: Emily\", \"B:  Michael\", \"C: Aiden\", \"D:  None of the speakers\"], \"question\": \"Who had their seat upgraded?\"}',2,'Option',10),(55,'{\"answer\": \"A\", \"options\": [\"A: Emily\", \"B:  Michael\", \"C: Aiden\", \"D:  None of the speakers\"], \"question\": \"Who boarded their plane before any other passengers?\"}',3,'Option',10),(56,'{\"answer\": \"D\", \"options\": [\"A: Emily\", \"B:  Michael\", \"C: Aiden\", \"D:  None of the speakers\"], \"question\": \"Who was injured while on their flight?\"}',4,'Option',10),(57,'{\"answer\": \"C\", \"options\": [\"A: Emily\", \"B:  Michael\", \"C: Aiden\", \"D:  None of the speakers\"], \"question\": \"Who arrived late for their flight?\"}',5,'Option',10),(58,'{\"answer\": \"A\", \"options\": [\"A: Emily\", \"B:  Michael\", \"C: Aiden\", \"D:  None of the speakers\"], \"question\": \"Who was mistakenly believed to be a child?\"}',6,'Option',10),(59,'{\"title\": \"Speaking test: sample\", \"videoUrl\": \"https://www.youtube.com/embed/SKwkmh8qExc\", \"instruction\": \"Watch this sample speaking test carefully. Pay close attention to the candidate\'s fluency, their use of varied vocabulary, and how they structure their responses to the examiner\'s prompts. Observe the body language and the interaction between both parties to better understand the pacing and formal expectations of the assessment.\"}',1,'Video',8),(60,'{\"answer\": \"True\", \"options\": [\"True\", \"False\"], \"question\": \"Did you watch the video?\"}',2,'True_False',8),(61,'{\"title\": \"Speaking test: sample\", \"videoUrl\": \"https://www.youtube.com/embed/KDCK-3gsxLY\", \"instruction\": \"Watch this sample speaking test carefully. Pay close attention to the candidate\'s fluency, their use of varied vocabulary, and how they structure their responses to the examiner\'s prompts. Observe the body language and the interaction between both parties to better understand the pacing and formal expectations of the assessment.\"}',1,'Video',12),(62,'{\"answer\": \"True\", \"options\": [\"True\", \"False\"], \"question\": \"Did you watch the video?\"}',2,'True_False',12),(63,'{\"text\": \"I went to London last week and it was (1) ____ amazing! We stayed (2) ____ a small hotel near the city center. We (3) ____ to see Big Ben and the London Eye on the first day. The weather was a bit (4) ____, but we didn\'t mind. I (5) ____ suggest visiting the museums because most of them are free. I am (6) ____ forward to going back again next year!\", \"title\": \"A Trip to London\", \"answer_key\": {\"1\": \"absolutely\", \"2\": \"at\", \"3\": \"managed\", \"4\": \"rainy\", \"5\": \"would\", \"6\": \"looking\"}, \"instruction\": \"Complete the blog post about a trip to London by choosing the best word for each gap.\", \"requirements\": {\"1\": [\"absolutely\", \"very\", \"enough\", \"too\"], \"2\": [\"at\", \"on\", \"to\", \"by\"], \"3\": [\"managed\", \"succeeded\", \"could\", \"arrived\"], \"4\": [\"rainy\", \"raining\", \"rains\", \"rain\"], \"5\": [\"would\", \"should\", \"must\", \"ought\"], \"6\": [\"looking\", \"seeing\", \"watching\", \"waiting\"]}}',1,'FillGaps',7),(64,'{\"text\": \"Are you interested in taking photos? If so, why not (1) ____ our new photography club? We meet every Wednesday (2) ____ 4 PM in Room 12. You don\'t (3) ____ to have a professional camera; a smartphone is enough. We will learn (4) ____ to take beautiful portraits and edit them. (5) ____ is a small fee of £2 per session to cover costs. I hope you can (6) ____ us this week!\", \"title\": \"The New Photography Club\", \"answer_key\": {\"1\": \"join\", \"2\": \"at\", \"3\": \"need\", \"4\": \"how\", \"5\": \"There\", \"6\": \"join\"}, \"instruction\": \"Read the announcement about a new school club and select the correct option for each gap.\", \"requirements\": {\"1\": [\"join\", \"enter\", \"go\", \"become\"], \"2\": [\"at\", \"on\", \"in\", \"for\"], \"3\": [\"need\", \"must\", \"should\", \"have\"], \"4\": [\"how\", \"what\", \"which\", \"way\"], \"5\": [\"There\", \"It\", \"This\", \"That\"], \"6\": [\"join\", \"meet\", \"stay\", \"find\"]}}',1,'FillGaps',11),(65,'{\"text\": \"The sudden shift to remote work has (1) ____ about significant changes in corporate culture. While many employees appreciate the flexibility, others argue that it (2) ____ a threat to team cohesion. It is of (3) ____ importance that managers find ways to maintain morale without the benefit of face-to-face interaction. Experts suggest that a hybrid model is likely to (4) ____ the most effective solution in the long run. However, we must (5) ____ in mind that not all industries are suited to this transition. Ultimately, the success of such a move (6) ____ on the ability of a company to foster trust digitally.\", \"title\": \"The Evolution of Remote Work\", \"answer_key\": {\"1\": \"brought\", \"2\": \"poses\", \"3\": \"paramount\", \"4\": \"prove\", \"5\": \"bear\", \"6\": \"hinges\"}, \"instruction\": \"Read the article about remote work and choose the word which best fits each gap.\", \"requirements\": {\"1\": [\"brought\", \"carried\", \"taken\", \"put\"], \"2\": [\"poses\", \"makes\", \"presents\", \"gives\"], \"3\": [\"paramount\", \"pivotal\", \"top\", \"main\"], \"4\": [\"prove\", \"turn\", \"result\", \"happen\"], \"5\": [\"bear\", \"keep\", \"hold\", \"carry\"], \"6\": [\"hinges\", \"resides\", \"bases\", \"dwells\"]}}',1,'FillGaps',27),(66,'{\"text\": \"The city council has recently (1) ____ an ambitious plan to rejuvenate the old industrial district. The project, which has been (2) ____ fire from local residents due to its cost, aims to transform derelict warehouses into luxury apartments. Architects claim that the development will (3) ____ new life into the area. However, there are concerns that local heritage might be (4) ____ for the sake of modernization. Despite the (5) ____ of the debate, the council seems (6) ____ on proceeding with the construction next month.\", \"title\": \"Urban Rejuvenation\", \"answer_key\": {\"1\": \"unveiled\", \"2\": \"under\", \"3\": \"breathe\", \"4\": \"sacrificed\", \"5\": \"intensity\", \"6\": \"intent\"}, \"instruction\": \"Read the text about city planning and select the most appropriate word for each gap.\", \"requirements\": {\"1\": [\"unveiled\", \"disclosed\", \"opened\", \"exposed\"], \"2\": [\"under\", \"on\", \"in\", \"by\"], \"3\": [\"breathe\", \"give\", \"inject\", \"send\"], \"4\": [\"sacrificed\", \"offered\", \"traded\", \"abandoned\"], \"5\": [\"intensity\", \"strength\", \"weight\", \"depth\"], \"6\": [\"intent\", \"fixed\", \"determined\", \"resolved\"]}}',1,'FillGaps',31),(68,'{\"text\": \"Many people today (1) ____ on social media to keep in touch with friends. However, some experts are concerned that it might have a negative (2) ____ on our social skills. For instance, we often spend more time looking at a screen (3) ____ than talking face-to-face. This can sometimes (4) ____ to a feeling of loneliness, even when we are connected online. It is important to (5) ____ limits on how much time we spend on our phones. After (6) ____, nothing can replace the value of a real, physical conversation.\", \"title\": \"The Impact of Social Media\", \"answer_key\": {\"1\": \"rely\", \"2\": \"impact\", \"3\": \"rather\", \"4\": \"lead\", \"5\": \"set\", \"6\": \"all\"}, \"instruction\": \"Read the text about social media and choose the word which best fits each gap.\", \"requirements\": {\"1\": [\"rely\", \"depend\", \"count\", \"believe\"], \"2\": [\"impact\", \"result\", \"change\", \"force\"], \"3\": [\"rather\", \"instead\", \"sooner\", \"better\"], \"4\": [\"lead\", \"cause\", \"make\", \"result\"], \"5\": [\"set\", \"make\", \"put\", \"give\"], \"6\": [\"all\", \"everything\", \"that\", \"time\"]}}',1,'FillGaps',15),(69,'{\"text\": \"Living in a big city can be exciting, but it also (1) ____ some significant challenges. Many young people are (2) ____ on the idea of having everything nearby, such as shops and theaters. However, it can be difficult to (3) ____ with the constant noise and pollution. On the other hand, if you live in a small village, you might feel (4) ____ out if there isn\'t much to do in the evenings. Most people find that (5) ____ as they have a good job and a circle of friends, they can be happy anywhere. It often (6) ____ down to personal preference.\", \"title\": \"Living in a Big City\", \"answer_key\": {\"1\": \"presents\", \"2\": \"keen\", \"3\": \"cope\", \"4\": \"left\", \"5\": \"as long\", \"6\": \"comes\"}, \"instruction\": \"Read the text about urban life and choose the best word for each gap.\", \"requirements\": {\"1\": [\"presents\", \"gives\", \"shows\", \"makes\"], \"2\": [\"keen\", \"fond\", \"interested\", \"enthusiastic\"], \"3\": [\"cope\", \"deal\", \"face\", \"manage\"], \"4\": [\"left\", \"cut\", \"put\", \"missed\"], \"5\": [\"as long\", \"provided\", \"unless\", \"although\"], \"6\": [\"comes\", \"goes\", \"gets\", \"falls\"]}}',1,'FillGaps',19),(70,'{\"text\": \"Learning a new skill is a journey that requires a great deal of (1) ____. At the beginning, you might feel like you are not making much (2) ____, but you shouldn\'t give up. It takes time to get (3) ____ to hearing different sounds or using new tools. One of the best ways to improve is to (4) ____ on practicing every day, even for just ten minutes. If you (5) ____ realistic goals for yourself, you will stay motivated throughout the process. Eventually, you will (6) ____ in communicating or performing at a high level.\", \"title\": \"Learning a New Skill\", \"answer_key\": {\"1\": \"effort\", \"2\": \"progress\", \"3\": \"used\", \"4\": \"carry\", \"5\": \"set\", \"6\": \"succeed\"}, \"instruction\": \"Read the article about lifelong learning and select the correct option for each gap.\", \"requirements\": {\"1\": [\"effort\", \"work\", \"try\", \"job\"], \"2\": [\"progress\", \"improvement\", \"gain\", \"increase\"], \"3\": [\"used\", \"familiar\", \"known\", \"aware\"], \"4\": [\"carry\", \"keep\", \"go\", \"hold\"], \"5\": [\"set\", \"make\", \"put\", \"draw\"], \"6\": [\"succeed\", \"manage\", \"achieve\", \"win\"]}}',1,'FillGaps',23),(71,'{\"title\": \"Speaking test: sample\", \"videoUrl\": \"https://www.youtube.com/embed/TdAVyz6GGQQ\", \"instruction\": \"Watch this sample speaking test carefully. Pay close attention to the candidate\'s fluency, their use of varied vocabulary, and how they structure their responses to the examiner\'s prompts. Observe the body language and the interaction between both parties to better understand the pacing and formal expectations of the assessment.\"}',1,'Video',16),(72,'{\"answer\": \"True\", \"options\": [\"True\", \"False\"], \"question\": \"Did you watch the video?\"}',2,'True_False',16),(73,'{\"title\": \"Speaking test: sample\", \"videoUrl\": \"https://www.youtube.com/embed/VUYctcmk51Y\", \"instruction\": \"Watch this sample speaking test carefully. Pay close attention to the candidate\'s fluency, their use of varied vocabulary, and how they structure their responses to the examiner\'s prompts. Observe the body language and the interaction between both parties to better understand the pacing and formal expectations of the assessment.\"}',1,'Video',20),(74,'{\"answer\": \"True\", \"options\": [\"True\", \"False\"], \"question\": \"Did you watch the video?\"}',2,'True_False',20),(75,'{\"title\": \"Speaking test: sample\", \"videoUrl\": \"https://www.youtube.com/embed/-tqeI9t4x9E\", \"instruction\": \"Watch this sample speaking test carefully. Pay close attention to the candidate\'s fluency, their use of varied vocabulary, and how they structure their responses to the examiner\'s prompts. Observe the body language and the interaction between both parties to better understand the pacing and formal expectations of the assessment.\"}',1,'Video',24),(76,'{\"answer\": \"True\", \"options\": [\"True\", \"False\"], \"question\": \"Did you watch the video?\"}',2,'True_False',24),(77,'{\"title\": \"Speaking test: sample\", \"videoUrl\": \"https://www.youtube.com/embed/5nGESyDgmdw\", \"instruction\": \"Watch this sample speaking test carefully. Pay close attention to the candidate\'s fluency, their use of varied vocabulary, and how they structure their responses to the examiner\'s prompts. Observe the body language and the interaction between both parties to better understand the pacing and formal expectations of the assessment.\"}',1,'Video',28),(78,'{\"answer\": \"True\", \"options\": [\"True\", \"False\"], \"question\": \"Did you watch the video?\"}',2,'True_False',28),(79,'{\"title\": \"Speaking test: sample\", \"videoUrl\": \"https://www.youtube.com/embed/XbIxcqOCiio\", \"instruction\": \"Watch this sample speaking test carefully. Pay close attention to the candidate\'s fluency, their use of varied vocabulary, and how they structure their responses to the examiner\'s prompts. Observe the body language and the interaction between both parties to better understand the pacing and formal expectations of the assessment.\"}',1,'Video',32),(80,'{\"answer\": \"True\", \"options\": [\"True\", \"False\"], \"question\": \"Did you watch the video?\"}',2,'True_False',32),(83,'{\"text\": \"As the climate crisis gets worse, millions of people are being displaced – forced to leave their homes – due to environmental problems. These people, often called climate migrants or climate refugees, face serious challenges. Climate change is causing many areas to become less safe or less able to support life. Some people abandon their homes because of sudden disasters like floods, hurricanes, or wildfires. Others must move because of slower changes like rising sea levels, which threaten entire islands and coastal cities, or because droughts make it hard to grow food.\\n\\nFor example, low-lying countries like Kiribati and Tuvalu in the Pacific Ocean are at risk of disappearing under the sea. Coastal communities in countries like Bangladesh and the United States are also experiencing flooding that makes life difficult. In agricultural areas, especially in places like sub-Saharan Africa, extreme droughts are reducing the amount of food that can be grown, forcing people to move to more livable areas. These types of migrations will likely increase as climate change continues to affect the world.\\n\\nOne of the biggest problems for climate migrants is that they do not have legal status under international law. Current laws, such as the 1951 Refugee Convention, protect people who are fleeing their countries due to war, persecution, or violence. However, these laws do not include climate change as a reason for seeking refuge; therefore, climate migrants do not receive the same protections or access to help as refugees. Climate immigration on such a scale is quite a recent phenomenon. For this reason, many countries lack clear policies for helping people displaced by natural disasters.\\n\\nIn response to this growing crisis, governments and organisations are working to provide aid and support to climate migrants. This includes emergency aid after disasters, as well as efforts to help communities adapt to changing environmental conditions so that people can remain in their homes. Humanitarian groups like the Red Cross and the United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees (UNHCR) are providing food, shelter, and medical care to people displaced by extreme weather events. These organisations play a key role in helping people recover after disasters, but long-lasting solutions are needed as well.\\n\\nSome efforts focus on adaptation, which means helping communities prepare for the effects of climate change. For example, in areas where floods are likely to happen, building better flood defences like strategic dams and stronger river banks, can allow people to stay in their homes. In drought-affected regions, introducing new types of crops that can survive with less water may help farmers continue to grow food. However, many countries lack the resources to implement these solutions on a large scale, so international support is needed. Another form of aid is resettlement programs, which allow climate migrants to move to other countries. However, these programs are often small and do not help enough people.\\n\\nAs natural disasters continue to displace millions of people, there is an urgent need for new legal protections for climate migrants. While some countries, like New Zealand, have started small programs to offer visas to the victims of extreme weather events, these programs are rare and limited. There are ongoing discussions at the United Nations about creating new legal frameworks to protect climate-displaced people, but they are taking a long time to move forward. At the same time, the world must work together to reduce the effects of climate change and help vulnerable communities adapt.\", \"title\": \"Climate Migrants\", \"instruction\": \"Read the text about climate migrants and for questions 1-6, choose the correct answer.\"}',1,'Text',13),(84,'{\"answer\": \"B\", \"options\": [\"A: choose to move to a country where natural disasters are less likely to happen.\", \"B:  have no other choice but to move because of the effects of climate change.\", \"C: move abroad in search of a better lifestyle and better work opportunities.\", \"D: are worried about the future of the environment in their own country.\"], \"question\": \"The writer describes as ‘climate migrants’ people who\"}',2,'Option',13),(85,'{\"answer\": \"D\", \"options\": [\"A: Existing infrastructure is unable to manage water resources from rivers, canals, and lakes.\", \"B: Extremely long periods of dry weather are having a devastating effect on agriculture.\", \"C: Excessive deforestation is causing frequent landslides near mountain communities.\", \"D: Expanding flooding threatens the land due to rising water levels in oceans, rivers, or lakes.\"], \"question\": \"According to the writer, what challenges do Kiribati and Tuvalu, Bangladesh, and the USA have in common?\"}',3,'Option',13),(86,'{\"answer\": \"C\", \"options\": [\"A: international laws related to the effects of climate change need to be updated.\", \"B: governments do not consider climate change a good enough reason for leaving a country.\", \"C: climate immigration is a recent issue that requires governments to create new laws.\", \"D: many countries refuse to help climate migrants in such large numbers.\"], \"question\": \"The writer suggests that the main challenge for climate migrants is that\"}',4,'Option',13),(87,'{\"answer\": \"A\", \"options\": [\"A: They carry out very important work, but it does not give long-term solutions.\", \"B: They will soon be unable to support the increasing number of climate migrants.\", \"C: They should focus their work on preventing natural disasters.\", \"D: They can’t control climate immigration without the support of the government.\"], \"question\": \"What does the writer imply about the UNHCR?\"}',5,'Option',13),(88,'{\"answer\": \"A\", \"options\": [\"A: Many countries need financial help from abroad to apply solutions to climate immigration.\", \"B: Many climate migrants are against resettlement programs that require moving abroad.\", \"C: Some flood-related issues are not considered dangerous enough to require any adaptation.\", \"D: Drought-related issues require changes that communities are not prepared to make.\"], \"question\": \"According to the writer, what is one challenge that makes it difficult to adapt to climate change?\"}',6,'Option',13),(95,'{\"text\": \"Cryptocurrency trading has become very popular. By buying and selling different currencies, traders can make huge profits. However, trading addiction is becoming increasingly common. Here, addict Josh Johnson talks about his experience.\\n\\n‘I heard about cryptocurrencies years ago when Bitcoin first came out, but I can’t say I was intrigued from the start. Even when cryptocurrencies were mentioned more and more in the news and on social media, I didn’t pay much attention. It wasn’t until my friends started trading that I got into it. Seeing them earning loads of money overnight by doing practically nothing made me want to get in on it too. I’d been trading for a while by the time the pandemic started, but that was when the trouble started.\\n\\n‘It was crazy to begin with. My first investment quadrupled within a couple of weeks. I couldn’t believe it. It gave me a feeling of power, and I felt as if I could achieve anything. I poured more money in, and the same thing kept happening. Of course, it was all chance. I happened to put my money in before the currencies hit an all-time high. Then they plummeted. I was convinced they’d recover, so I kept pumping more money in. But what had worked before no longer did. I lost everything.\\n\\n‘Since getting help, I’ve thought about how I got addicted. There was a point when I had over three hundred thousand pounds. If I’d quit then, I could have bought the house that I had my heart set on. But rather than the money itself, it was the drama that hooked me. And winning didn’t give me as big a thrill as losing. You would think I’d feel devastated when money disappeared, but instead, I’d feel a new sense of purpose and drive to prove myself once again.\\n\\n‘Lots of people enjoy these highs and lows of trading without becoming addicts, so it’s hard to know when you’ve gone too far. Like with many addictions, crypto addicts become isolated and withdrawn and neglect their work. But I was off work and living by myself, so if those signs were there, they weren’t noticeable. For me, I missed the high when money ran out and I couldn’t trade, so I found it through drugs, and my sister found out. I’d been addicted to them in the past, and it got to the point of me lying and stealing from others. She didn’t want that to happen again, and she stepped in.\\n\\n‘There’s a worrying, rising trend in cryptocurrency trading addiction. It’s not surprising really, given the irresponsible advertising techniques which companies have used. I’m glad authorities are cracking down on those. But it concerns me that the apps send you notifications when you make money, inviting you to share your good news with others. But when you lose, you get nothing. It gives you a false sense that you are doing well. I think there should be rules that don’t allow that.\\n\\n‘Compared to traditional gambling, cryptocurrency trading is seen as socially acceptable. It’s something that smart but adventurous people do. And whereas a lot of gamblers play alone or against other people, trading is social. But this leads to a herd mentality, where people stop thinking things through for themselves. The companies make lots of information available to educate people about the risks of trading. It’s far easier, though, to act on a friend’s tip-off or hunch. All this means that people don’t realise that they are on a slippery slope until it’s gone too far.’\", \"title\": \"Crypto addicts\", \"instruction\": \"Read the article about cryptocurrency addiction and answer the questions that follow.\"}',1,'Text',17),(96,'{\"answer\": \"C\", \"options\": [\"A: when Bitcoin first came out.\", \"B: after seeing it in the media.\", \"C: influenced by his peers\' success\", \"D: while working from home during the pandemic.\"], \"question\": \"Josh started trading in cryptocurrencies...\"}',2,'Option',17),(97,'{\"answer\": \"C\", \"options\": [\"A: He immediately lost all the money he invested.\", \"B: He made many small wins and slightly larger losses.\", \"C: He made lots of money, lost it all and couldn’t regain it.\", \"D: He recovered from big losses at first but later could not.\"], \"question\": \"What was Josh’s experience while trading?\"}',3,'Option',17),(98,'{\"answer\": \"D\", \"options\": [\"A: he wanted enough money to buy a house.\", \"B: he enjoyed the excitement of winning money.\", \"C: he didn’t want to confess that he’d lost money.\", \"D: he enjoyed the feeling he got when he lost money.\"], \"question\": \"Josh says that he kept gambling because...\"}',4,'Option',17),(99,'{\"answer\": \"C\", \"options\": [\"A: he stopped working and spending time with other people.\", \"B: he started to sell drugs in order to fund his trading habit.\", \"C: he returned to a different, former addiction.\", \"D: he started telling lies and taking things from family remembers.\"], \"question\": \"For Josh, crypto currency trading became a problem when...\"}',5,'Option',17),(100,'{\"answer\": \"B\", \"options\": [\"A: cryptocurrencies are advertised.\", \"B: trading apps communicate with traders.\", \"C: trading apps encourage sharing among friends.\", \"D: cryptocurrency companies are regulated.\"], \"question\": \"Josh would like to see a change in the way that...\"}',6,'Option',17),(101,'{\"text\": \"There is increasing evidence that health is linked to personality. However, until now, the relationship has not affected the way health care is delivered. There are several reasons for this. Some health workers doubt whether there is a direct link between health and personality or whether it’s just a coincidence. Some feel it is their professional duty to treat all patients in the same way. Others argue that delivering health services according to patients’ personalities will have minimal impact and therefore isn’t worth the effort. However, some psychologists believe that applying different procedures to people with different personalities could have a significant, positive effect on health.\\n\\nResearch into personality has, in recent years, focused on the Big Five model of personality types. This model measures how neurotic, extrovert, open to experience, agreeable and conscientious a person is. Some of these personality types have been studied in relation to health. For example, conscientious people tend to be less likely to smoke, drink too much alcohol or be inactive. However, in other cases, the relationship is less clear. Neurotic behaviour, for instance, has been found in some studies to increase the risk of death, in others to protect people from illness and in others to have no link to health at all.\\n\\nEven so, if health workers applied an understanding of personality to the services they provide, they could influence the extent to which patients act on advice and follow their treatment. For example, high sensation-seeking individuals, who are extroverts and unconscientious in the Big Five model and tend to take part in risky activities, respond to drama, energy and emotion. Thus, to encourage those people to follow health advice, health promotions can be designed to incorporate those factors. An example of this was the campaign SENTAR which aimed to reduce cannabis use among high sensation-seeking teenagers. By creating a suitable television advert, they successfully engaged these youths and reduced their recreational drug use. Of course, this approach isn’t always possible. It is often impractical and expensive to create several versions of a campaign to reach different personality types. However, recent developments in computer technology, cookies and targeted advertising may allow this approach to be used more in future.\\n\\nPersonality could also be considered when sending messages, information and guidance to specific patients. Already, health information is usually available in various forms – printed, digital, audio, and so on – to be suitable and accessible for different users, such as the blind, the elderly, and people with reading difficulties. Research has also shown that, by identifying different patients’ motivations for treatment and then corresponding with them in a way that reflects their motivations, patients will become more involved in their treatment, compared to when the same messages are sent to everyone. Correspondence could, therefore, be adapted to reflect patients’ personality type, too. For example, less conscientious people could be sent phone reminders to attend appointments. So far, there has been very little research into the effectiveness of tailoring health guidance according to personality, so this area deserves further study.\\n\\nUntil now, the focus of personality-health research has been to explore the link between personality and health and has had very little practical application. Thus, health workers have not engaged deeply with it. However, by suggesting, trialling and implementing practices to engage patients with different personalities, the relationship between psychology researchers and health workers could improve, along with the health of the general public.\", \"title\": \"Personality and health\", \"instruction\": \"Read the article about the link between personality and health care, then answer the questions that follow.\"}',1,'Text',21),(102,'{\"answer\": \"A\", \"options\": [\"A: health workers outside psychology\", \"B: psychologists\", \"C: neurotic people\", \"D: patients at a clinic\"], \"question\": \"Who is the article most likely aimed at?\"}',2,'Option',21),(103,'{\"answer\": \"C\", \"options\": [\"A: has shown that sensation-seeking individuals often risk their health.\", \"B: should be carried out by both clinicians and psychologists.\", \"C: can be practically applied to improve public health.\", \"D: has not been studied in great depth until recently.\"], \"question\": \"What is the main idea of the article? Research into the link between Health and Personality...\"}',3,'Option',21),(104,'{\"answer\": \"D\", \"options\": [\"A: They think the effect on a patient’s health will be hardly noticeable.\", \"B: They consider it their duty to treat all patients equally.\", \"C: They doubt whether a person’s personality directly affects their health.\", \"D: They lack sufficient training in psychology.\"], \"question\": \"Which of these is NOT a reason why clinicians do not currently consider personality in their approach to healthcare?\"}',4,'Option',21),(105,'{\"answer\": \"B\", \"options\": [\"A: They are at greater risk from early death than non-neurotic patients.\", \"B: There is no consistent link between a patient’s level of neurosis and their health.\", \"C: They are more likely than non-neurotic patients to report illness.\", \"D: Their neurosis protects them from becoming sick.\"], \"question\": \"What can be concluded from the text about neurotic patients?\"}',5,'Option',21),(106,'{\"answer\": \"C\", \"options\": [\"A: Explore the link between personality and health.\", \"B: Do more research before giving advice to health workers.\", \"C: Give more practical suggestions to health workers.\", \"D: Talk directly to the general public.\"], \"question\": \"In the final paragraph, what does the writer advise researchers in health/personality to do?\"}',6,'Option',21),(107,'{\"text\": \"We all love a good laugh, but have you ever tried to explain why something is funny? It\'s surprisingly difficult. The moment you start analysing a joke, it dies on the spot — like a frog in biology class. Still, humour fascinates us because it sits somewhere between logic and madness: there’s order in the chaos, sense in the silliness. And when a joke really lands, we burst out laughing, unable to stop ourselves.\\n\\nAccording to psychologists, most laughter begins when our brain spots something unanticipated — a twist, a mismatch, a sudden change of direction. It\'s that instant when we think, ‘Wait, what just happened?’ and then realise everything still makes sense. The surprise releases tension, and boom — we laugh. A punchline is basically a friendly ambush.\\n\\nBut the science only tells half the story. Laughter isn\'t a solo sport; it\'s contagious. We are thirty times more likely to laugh when we\'re with other people than when we\'re alone. You can sit through a comedy special in silent amusement, but stick you in a crowd and suddenly you\'re snorting like a donkey. Laughter connects us — it\'s a social signal that says, ‘We\'re getting each other.’\\n\\nAnd, of course, humour is deeply cultural. British people love irony, understatement — playing down the importance of something — and self-deprecation, making themselves the butt of the joke. Americans, on the other hand, prefer stories, anecdotes and exaggeration. Then there\'s sarcasm, wordplay and absurdity — a full menu of comedy styles. The problem is, jokes don\'t always travel well. A pun that brings the house down in London might leave a room in Lisbon completely silent, making even the comedian cringe at the awkward silence.\\n\\nBut perhaps the most admirable kind of humour is laughing at ourselves. Self-deprecating jokes show confidence, not weakness. They turn embarrassment into charm. When we can see the funny side of our own mistakes, we stop pretending to be perfect — and that\'s something everyone can relate to.\\n\\nHumour also has its darker corners. Some jokes cross a line and start to offend people; others simply fall flat because the timing and tone are not on point — what sounds hilarious in a pub might seem cruel in a meeting. As any stand-up will tell you, the difference between ‘you killed it’ and ‘you died on stage’ can come down to just one poorly timed pause.\\n\\nSo, what makes something truly funny? It isn\'t just clever writing or perfect timing. It\'s that spark of shared recognition — the moment when we all laugh and think, ‘Yes, that\'s so true.’ Because deep down, every good joke reminds us that being human is both ridiculous… and wonderful.\", \"title\": \"What makes us laugh?\", \"instruction\": \"Read the article about the science and social aspects of humor, then answer the questions that follow.\"}',1,'Text',25),(108,'{\"answer\": \"B\", \"options\": [\"A: How difficult it is to recreate laughter.\", \"B: That it\'s hard to explain why we laugh.\", \"C: That some jokes make no sense.\"], \"question\": \"What is the author trying to explain with the frog metaphor?\"}',2,'Option',25),(109,'{\"answer\": \"A\", \"options\": [\"A: An unexpected turn of events.\", \"B: An expected surprise.\", \"C: A moment of confusion.\"], \"question\": \"What would be a punchline, according to the author?\"}',3,'Option',25),(110,'{\"answer\": \"B\", \"options\": [\"A: You often don\'t find comedy funny while alone.\", \"B: Laughing is a bonding experience.\", \"C: We laugh 30 times as much when we aren\'t alone.\"], \"question\": \"What does the author imply in paragraph 3?\"}',4,'Option',25),(111,'{\"answer\": \"C\", \"options\": [\"A: Well, cannibalism is a bit frowned upon in this area.\", \"B:  I chose to do comedy because everybody said I\'m a joke\", \"C: He was so fat that he fell off the bed on both sides at the same time.\"], \"question\": \"Which of these lines wouldn\'t sound like typical British humour according to the text?\"}',5,'Option',25),(112,'{\"answer\": \"A\", \"options\": [\"A:  Humour is fragile and can easily fail if delivered badly.\", \"B:  Timing is the most important part of performing comedy.\", \"C: People should be careful where they tell certain jokes.\"], \"question\": \"What does the author mean by the \'darker corners\' of humour?\"}',6,'Option',25),(113,'{\"text\": \"In the modern digital landscape, the most valuable currency is no longer data or oil, but human attention. This concept, known as the Attention Economy, suggests that because human attention is a limited resource, tech companies must compete fiercely to capture and held it. As we navigate through apps and websites, we aren\'t just consumers; we are the product being sold to advertisers. The longer a platform can keep a user scrolling, the more revenue it generates, leading to a design philosophy that prioritizes engagement over well-being.\\n\\nTo achieve this, developers employ psychological triggers rooted in behavioral science. One of the most effective tools is the \'variable reward\' schedule—the same mechanism that makes slot machines so addictive. When we pull down to refresh a social media feed, we don\'t know if we will see a liked photo, an interesting news story, or nothing at all. This uncertainty triggers a dopamine hit in the brain, compelled us to check our devices repeatedly throughout the day. These features are not accidental; they are precisely engineered to bypass our conscious willpower.\\n\\nHowever, the consequences of this constant stimulation are becoming increasingly apparent. Psychologists point to a phenomenon called \'continuous partial attention,\' where individuals are perpetually connected but never fully present. This state of high alertness leads to increased stress levels and a significant decline in deep, concentrated work. When our focus is fragmented by constant notifications and the lure of infinite scrolls, our ability to engage with complex ideas or maintain long-term memory is compromised.\\n\\nCritics of the Attention Economy argue that it erodes the very fabric of social discourse. Algorithms are designed to show us content that provokes a strong emotional reaction, as outrage and excitement are more likely to keep us engaged than nuance or calm reporting. This often results in the creation of echo chambers, where users are only exposed to information that confirms their existing biases, further polarizing society. The business model, it seems, is at odds with the goal of an informed and cohesive public.\\n\\nIn response, a growing movement is advocating for \'Time Well Spent\' and ethical design. These advocates call for technology that respects our boundaries and helps us achieve our goals rather than exploiting our psychological vulnerabilities. Some governments are also considering regulations to limit persuasive design patterns, such as autoplay features and infinite scrolling. Ultimately, regaining control over our attention may require both a shift in corporate responsibility and a personal commitment to digital minimalism—learning to disconnect in order to truly reconnect with the world around us.\", \"title\": \"The Attention Economy\", \"instruction\": \"Read the article about how digital media captures human attention, then answer the questions that follow.\"}',1,'Text',29),(114,'{\"answer\": \"B\", \"options\": [\"A: Data has become less valuable than physical commodities like oil.\", \"B: Companies now prioritize user attention over traditional product sales.\", \"C: Users are increasingly seen as active participants rather than passive consumers.\"], \"question\": \"What is the primary shift in the digital marketplace?\"}',2,'Option',29),(115,'{\"answer\": \"C\", \"options\": [\"A: The financial risk involved in using digital platforms.\", \"B: The technical complexity of modern app development.\", \"C: The psychological pull created by unpredictable rewards.\"], \"question\": \"The author compares social media feeds to slot machines to illustrate:\"}',3,'Option',29),(116,'{\"answer\": \"B\", \"options\": [\"A: They are capable of multitasking more effectively than previous generations.\", \"B: Their focus is spread thin, preventing deep engagement with tasks.\", \"C: They are constantly seeking out new information to improve their memory.\"], \"question\": \"What does the term \'continuous partial attention\' imply about modern users?\"}',4,'Option',29),(117,'{\"answer\": \"B\", \"options\": [\"A: A more informed public that understands nuanced political issues.\", \"B: The reinforcement of existing beliefs and increased social division.\", \"C: A decrease in the amount of time people spend discussing controversial topics.\"], \"question\": \"What is a major social consequence of algorithms that prioritize emotional engagement?\"}',5,'Option',29),(118,'{\"answer\": \"A\", \"options\": [\"A: Be designed to align with the user’s actual goals and well-being.\", \"B: Be regulated by governments to ensure it remains free of charge.\", \"C: Focus on making infinite scrolling more educational for younger users.\"], \"question\": \"The \'Time Well Spent\' movement suggests that technology should:\"}',6,'Option',29),(125,'{\"title\": \"Can music be medicine?\", \"videoUrl\": \"https://www.youtube.com/embed/LEyiYbkNbHg\", \"instruction\": \"Watch the video and answer the questions below:\"}',1,'Video',14),(126,'{\"answer\": \"B\", \"options\": [\"A: It relies on listening to your favourite music.\", \"B: It is designed to improve mental and physical health.\", \"C: It\'s entertainment used for healing.\"], \"question\": \"What does Sarah say to describe music therapy?\"}',2,'Option',14),(127,'{\"answer\": \"B\", \"options\": [\"A: Casual listening does not affect people\'s emotions.\", \"B: Music therapy requires guidance.\", \"C: Both are equally effective for therapy.\"], \"question\": \"What best distinguishes music therapy from casual listening?\"}',3,'Option',14),(128,'{\"answer\": \"C\", \"options\": [\"A: Only people with emotional issues or brain injuries.\", \"B: People suffering from anxiety and insomnia.\", \"C: People with mental health or mobility issues.\"], \"question\": \"According to Sarah, who can music therapy support?\"}',4,'Option',14),(129,'{\"answer\": \"A\", \"options\": [\"A: She improved her mobility and speech.\", \"B: Singing helped her walk correctly again.\", \"C: She fully recovered her speech.\"], \"question\": \"What progress did Mia make thanks to music therapy?\"}',5,'Option',14),(130,'{\"answer\": \"C\", \"options\": [\"A: It helps people be more focused.\", \"B: Angry people can listen to an old favourite song and calm down.\", \"C: Playing an instrument can help with anger or frustration.\"], \"question\": \"What emotional effects can music therapy have on patients?\"}',6,'Option',14),(131,'{\"title\": \"Student accommodation\", \"videoUrl\": \"https://www.youtube.com/embed/Lig4LOsShok\", \"instruction\": \"Watch the video and answer the questions below:\"}',1,'Video',18),(132,'{\"answer\": \"A\", \"options\": [\"A: Jennie\", \"B: Ben\", \"C: Joseph\", \"D: None of the speakers\"], \"question\": \"Who would have preferred a different type of accommodation?\"}',2,'Option',18),(133,'{\"answer\": \"C\", \"options\": [\"A: Jennie\", \"B: Ben\", \"C: Joseph\", \"D: None of the speakers\"], \"question\": \"Who did not get on well with the people he/she lived with?\"}',3,'Option',18),(134,'{\"answer\": \"D\", \"options\": [\"A: Jennie\", \"B: Ben\", \"C: Joseph\", \"D: None of the speakers\"], \"question\": \"Who lived a long distance from the university campus?\"}',4,'Option',18),(135,'{\"answer\": \"B\", \"options\": [\"A: Jennie\", \"B: Ben\", \"C: Joseph\", \"D: None of the speakers\"], \"question\": \"Who did not manage his/her budget well?\"}',5,'Option',18),(136,'{\"answer\": \"C\", \"options\": [\"A: Jennie\", \"B: Ben\", \"C: Joseph\", \"D: None of the speakers\"], \"question\": \"Who moved accommodation during the first year?\"}',6,'Option',18),(137,'{\"title\": \"The personality of centenarians\", \"videoUrl\": \"https://www.youtube.com/embed/q6W0RPSBP88\", \"instruction\": \"Watch the video and answer the questions below:\"}',1,'Video',22),(138,'{\"answer\": \"True\", \"options\": [\"True\", \"False\"], \"question\": \"Centenarians are inclined to talk about their feelings.\"}',2,'True_False',22),(139,'{\"answer\": \"False\", \"options\": [\"True\", \"False\"], \"question\": \"In order to carry out their study, they tried to find centenarians who had a positive personality.\"}',3,'True_False',22),(140,'{\"answer\": \"False\", \"options\": [\"True\", \"False\"], \"question\": \"They found that a positive attitude towards life is the secret of longevity.\"}',4,'True_False',22),(141,'{\"answer\": \"True\", \"options\": [\"True\", \"False\"], \"question\": \"They wanted to study whether positive personality was in the genes of those who live longest.\"}',5,'True_False',22),(142,'{\"answer\": \"True\", \"options\": [\"True\", \"False\"], \"question\": \"They found that the positive personality traits common in older people are associated with age more than with genes.\"}',6,'True_False',22),(143,'{\"title\": \"When I completely embarrassed myself\", \"videoUrl\": \"https://www.youtube.com/embed/V7bOxiNM3kc\", \"instruction\": \"Watch the video and answer the questions below:\"}',1,'Video',26),(144,'{\"answer\": \"C\", \"options\": [\"A: Fiona and her boyfriend went out because they needed milk, tea, and bread.\", \"B: They tried to find a parking spot for a long time.\", \"C: Fiona\'s boyfriend drove around the shop building.\"], \"question\": \"Which sentence is true?\"}',2,'Option',26),(145,'{\"answer\": \"A\", \"options\": [\"A: Fiona got into a moving car.\", \"B: The driver knew Fiona but didn\'t recognise her.\", \"C: Fiona wanted to prank her boyfriend.\"], \"question\": \"Which sentence is true?\"}',3,'Option',26),(146,'{\"answer\": \"B\", \"options\": [\"A: Because the driver was looking back.\", \"B: Because he accelerated in panic.\", \"C: Because he didn\'t see the car in front had stopped.\"], \"question\": \"Why did the car crash?\"}',4,'Option',26),(147,'{\"answer\": \"B\", \"options\": [\"A: Amanda is the manager of a big team.\", \"B: John wanted to give the shirt to someone.\", \"C: John couldn\'t attend due to health issues.\"], \"question\": \"Which sentence is true?\"}',5,'Option',26),(148,'{\"answer\": \"B\", \"options\": [\"A: Amanda sent John an XL shirt.\", \"B: She misspelled a word, which completely changed her message.\", \"C: John had an emergency.\"], \"question\": \"Which sentence is true?\"}',6,'Option',26),(149,'{\"title\": \"Why is walking humans’ superpower?\", \"videoUrl\": \"https://www.youtube.com/embed/YcsKLQRmIaU\", \"instruction\": \"Watch the video and answer the questions below:\"}',1,'Video',30),(150,'{\"answer\": \"A\", \"options\": [\"A: How humans moved, thought, and socialised.\", \"B: How humans moved and communicated.\", \"C: How humans moved, thought and planned.\"], \"question\": \"According to Dr Hammond, what did bipedal walking change?\"}',2,'Option',30),(151,'{\"answer\": \"A\", \"options\": [\"A: The savannah theory\", \"B: The energy-efficiency theory\", \"C: The carrying theory\"], \"question\": \"Which theory says safety influenced the transition to bipedalism?\"}',3,'Option',30),(152,'{\"answer\": \"C\", \"options\": [\"A: True\", \"B: False\", \"C: Not mentioned\"], \"question\": \"There\'s unlikely to be one single true theory.\"}',4,'Option',30),(153,'{\"answer\": \"B\", \"options\": [\"A: The savannah theory\", \"B: The energy-efficiency theory\", \"C: The carrying theory\"], \"question\": \"Which theory helps explain how human intelligence developed?\"}',5,'Option',30),(154,'{\"answer\": \"B\", \"options\": [\"A: Our genes are adapting to walk less.\", \"B: Walking might be beneficial for people with depression.\", \"C: Creative people tend to walk more.\"], \"question\": \"Which of the following can be inferred from what Dr Hammond says?\"}',6,'Option',30);
